            apTip: "A strong literary analysis thesis should make a SPECIFIC, ARGUABLE interpretive claim (not simply a summary or an obvious, non-debatable statement) — and the rest of the essay should THOROUGHLY DEVELOP and SUPPORT this claim using specific, well-analyzed textual evidence, demonstrating the kind of original, evidence-based interpretive thinking that the AP Lit exam's essays are specifically designed to evaluate and reward."
